:.9em;line-height:1.4;margin:0 auto 4px auto;width:100%}.mw-parser-output .wst-header-left{float:left}</style><div class=\"ws-header wst-header-structure wst-unknown wst-header ws-header ws-noexport noprint dynlayout-exempt\"><div class=\"wst-header-mainblock wst-unknown-mainblock headertemplate\"><div class=\"wst-header-back searchaux wst-unknown-back\"><div class=\"wst-header-back-arrow\">\u2190</div><div class=\"wst-header-back-link\"><a href=\"/wiki/The_Discourses_of_Epictetus;_with_the_Encheiridion_and_Fragments/Book_2/Chapter_2\" title=\"The Discourses of Epictetus; with the Encheiridion and Fragments/Book 2/Chapter 2\">Book 2, Chapter 2</a></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-central-cell wst-unknown-central-cell\"><span class=\"wst-header-title wst-unknown-title\"><span class=\"wst-header-title-text\"><a href=\"/wiki/The_Discourses_of_Epictetus;_with_the_Encheiridion_and_Fragments\" title=\"The Discourses of Epictetus; with the Encheiridion and Fragments\">The Discourses of Epictetus; with the Encheiridion and Fragments</a></span> (<span class=\"wst-header-year-text\">1877</span>)<br class=\"wst-header-title-break\" /><span class=\"contributor-text\">by&#160;<span class=\"fn\"><a href=\"/wiki/Author:Epictetus\" title=\"Author:Epictetus\">Epictetus</a></span></span>, <span class=\"contributor-text\">translated by&#160;<span class=\"fn\"><a href=\"/wiki/Author:George_Long\" title=\"Author:George Long\">George Long</a></span></span><div class=\"header-section-text\">Book 2, Chapter 3</div></span></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward searchaux wst-unknown-forward\"><div class=\"wst-header-forward-link\"><a href=\"/wiki/The_Discourses_of_Epictetus;_with_the_Encheiridion_and_Fragments/Book_2/Chapter_4\" title=\"The Discourses of Epictetus; with the Encheiridion and Fragments/Book 2/Chapter 4\">Book 2, Chapter 4</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-author\">Epictetus</span><span id=\"ws-article-id\">4535177</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/The_Discourses_of_Epictetus;_with_the_Encheiridion_and_Fragments\" title=\"The Discourses of Epictetus; with the Encheiridion and Fragments\">The Discourses of Epictetus; with the Encheiridion and Fragments</a> \u2014 Book 2, Chapter 3</span><span id=\"ws-year\">1877</span><span id=\"ws-translator\">George Long</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 1</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r13049624/mw-parser-output/.prp-pages-output\">.mw-parser-output .prp-pages-output .wst-heading{font-size:120%;text-align:center;margin-top:2em}</style><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"106\" data-page-number=\"106\" data-page-name=\"Page:Discourses of Epictetus.djvu/160\" data-page-index=\"160\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Discourses&#95;of&#95;Epictetus.djvu/160\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;160\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span>\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15620827\">.mw-parser-output .wst-center{text-align:center;margin:0 auto}.mw-parser-output .wst-center.wst-center-nomargin>p{margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0}</style><div class=\"wst-center tiInherit\">\n<p><span style=\"font-size:120%;\">CHAPTER III.</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r15620827\" /><div class=\"wst-center tiInherit\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">to those who recommend persons to philosophers.</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">Diogenes</span> said well to one who asked from him letters of recommendation, \"That you are a man, he said, he will know as soon as he sees you; and he will know whether you are good or bad, if he is by experience skilful to distinguish the good and the bad; but if he is without experience, he will never know, if I write to him ten thousand times.\"<sup id=\"cite&#95;ref-1\" class=\"reference\"><a href=\"#cite_note-1\"><span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#91;</span>1<span class=\"cite-bracket\">&#93;</span></a></sup> For it is just the same as if a drachma (a piece of silver money) asked to be recommended to a person to be tested. If he is skilful in testing silver, he will know what you are, for you (the drachma) will recommend yourself. We ought then in life also to have some skill as in the case of silver coin that a man may be able to say like the judge of silver, Bring me any drachma and I will test it. But in the case of syllogisms, I would say, Bring any man that you please, and I will distinguish for you the man who knows how to resolve syllogisms and the man who does not. Why? Because I know how to resolve syllogisms. I have the power, which a man must have who is able to discover those who have the power of resolving syllogisms. But in life how do I act? At one time I call a thing good, and at another time bad. What is the reason? The contrary to that which is in the case of syllogisms, ignorance and inexperience.\n</p>\n<div class=\"&#95;&#95;nop wst-nop\"></div>\n<p>&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"mw-heading mw-heading2\"><h2 id=\"Footnotes\">Footnotes</h2><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/w/index.php?title=The_Discourses_of_Epictetus;_with_the_Encheiridion_and_Fragments/Book_2/Chapter_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Footnotes\"><span>edit</span></a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></div>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14922110\">.mw-parser-output .wst-smallrefs{font-size:83%;clear:both;line-height:1.25;margin-top:0.3em}.mw-parser-output .wst-smallrefs ol.references{margin-top:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-smallrefs-ruled{border-top:1px solid;padding-top:0.3em}.mw-parser-output .wst-smallrefs ol.references.uksi{margin-top:0;list-style-type:lower-alpha}.mw-parser-output .wst-smallrefs ol.references.uksi li::marker{font-weight:bold}</style>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\"><div class=\"reflist wst-smallrefs\" style=\"\">\n<ol class=\"references\">\n<li id=\"cite&#95;note-1\"><span class=\"mw-cite-backlink\"><a href=\"#cite_ref-1\">\u2191</a></span> <span class=\"reference-text\">Mrs. Carter says 'This is one of the many extravagant refinements of the philosophers; and might lead persons into very dangerous mistakes, if it was laid down as a maxim in ordinary life.' I think that Mrs. Carter has not seen the meaning of Epictetus. The philosopher will discover the man's character by trying him, as the assayer tries the silver by a test.<br />Cicero (De legibus, i. 9) says that the face expresses the hidden character. Euripides (Medea, 518) says better, that no mark is impressed on the body by which we can distinguish the good man from the bad. Shakspere says<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431465\">.mw-parser-output .wst-block-center{display:block;margin:0 auto;padding:0;position:relative;max-width:100%;height:auto;text-align:initial}.mw-parser-output .wst-block-center-title{display:inline-block;width:100%;text-align:center}</style><div class=\"wst-block-center\" style=\"width:fit-content;\">\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431454\">.mw-parser-output .wst-size-block{line-height:1.4}.mw-parser-output .wst-size-block p{font-size:inherit}.mw-parser-output .wst-xx-smaller{font-size:58%}.mw-parser-output .wst-x-smaller{font-size:69%}.mw-parser-output .wst-smaller{font-size:83%}.mw-parser-output .wst-fine{line-height:1.4;font-size:92%}.mw-parser-output .wst-m-larger{font-size:110%}.mw-parser-output .wst-larger{font-size:120%}.mw-parser-output .wst-x-larger{font-size:144%}.mw-parser-output .wst-xx-larger{font-size:182%}.mw-parser-output .wst-xxx-larger{font-size:207%}.mw-parser-output .wst-xxxx-larger{font-size:249%}</style><div class=\"wst-size-block wst-fine wst-fine-block\">\n<p><span class=\"wst-gap &#95;&#95;gap\" style=\"display:inline-block; inline-size:10em\"></span>There's no art<br />To find the mind's construction in the face.<br /><span class=\"wst-gap &#95;&#95;gap\" style=\"display:inline-block; inline-size:12em\"></span>Macbeth, act i. sc. 4\n</p>\n</div>\n</div></span>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n</div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nParsed by mw\u2010web.eqiad.main\u201068596b847\u2010pj6bg\nCached time: 20260701173951\nCache expiry: 2592000\nCache expiry source: Module:Header (os.date(%Y))\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [vary\u2010page\u2010id, vary\u2010revision\u2010sha1]\nCPU time usage: 0.169 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.361 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 408/1000000\nRevision size: 487/2097152 bytes\nPost\u2010expand include size: 8221/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 1293/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 13/100\nExpensive parser function count: 4/500\nUnstrip recursion depth: 1/20\nUnstrip post\u2010expand size: 7751/5000000 bytes\nLua time usage: 0.066/10.000 seconds\nLua memory usage: 1284162/52428800 bytes\nNumber of Wikibase entities loaded: 0/500\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%  349.240      1 -total\n 49.23%  171.942      1 Template:Header\n 22.44%   78.371      1 Template:Smallrefs\n 17.32%   60.487      1 Template:Center_block\n 12.43%   43.412      2 Template:C\n 10.37%   36.232      1 Template:Fine_block\n  6.34%   22.129      1 Template:Fine_block/s\n  4.61%   16.089      1 Template:Block_center/s\n  4.31%   15.036      1 Template:Font_size_block/s\n  4.16%   14.522      2 Template:Center/s\n-->\n\n<!-- Render ID dcb90fc3-7573-11f1-99ce-8301ba4cd037 -->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key enwikisource:pcache:4535177:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20260701173951 and revision id 14267781. Rendering was triggered because: page_view\n -->\n</div>"}}